  "provider_query": "Public Telegram web preview of the official Telegram channel",
  "answer": "The official Telegram channel is **Telegram News** (@telegram). Its public web preview (no app or login required) is at **https://t.me/s/telegram**.\n\nRelevant results:\n\n1. **Public channel preview** — https://t.me/s/telegram — “Telegram News,” verified, ~9.33M subscribers. Description: “The official Telegram on Telegram. Much recursion. Very Telegram. Wow.” Recent posts cover product updates (e.g. welcome messages, buttons in messages, communities, smartwatch apps).  \n   [[1]](https://t.me/s/telegram)\n\n2. **Username link** — https://t.me/telegram — opens the same channel in the browser preview or the Telegram app.  \n   [[2]](https://telegram.org/faq_channels)\n\n3. **Channels FAQ** — https://telegram.org/faq_channels — points to the official channel as t.me/telegram and explains that public channels are viewable on the web.  \n   [[2]](https://telegram.org/faq_channels)\n\n4. **Channels tour** — https://telegram.org/tour/channels — says public channel content can be seen on the web without an account (example pattern: t.me/s/…) and cites the official @Telegram channel.  \n   [[3]](https://telegram.org/tour/channels/?setln=id)\n\n5. **Post embed docs** — https://core.telegram.org/widgets/post — how public channel posts (including this channel) can be embedded from their t.me links.  \n   [[4]](https://core.telegram.org/widgets/post)\n\n6. **Deep links** — https://core.telegram.org/api/links — t.me username and message-link format used for public channel previews.  \n   [[5]](https://core.telegram.org/api/links)\n\n7. **Telegram blog** — https://telegram.org/blog — full write-ups of the updates announced on the channel.  \n   [[6]](https://telegram.org/blog)\n\n8. **Alternate preview snapshots** — same channel under https://t.me/s/TELEGRAM and https://t.me/s/Telegram (case variants of the username).  \n   [[7]](https://t.me/s/TELEGRAM?before=266)",
